WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.099 --> 00:00:05.250
So it's Thursday, meaning we once again have
new unemployment numbers from last week.

00:00:05.250 --> 00:00:07.700
The numbers are not good.

00:00:07.700 --> 00:00:12.860
2.4 million more people filed for unemployment
benefits last week.

00:00:12.860 --> 00:00:17.290
And this in total now it's starting to be
staggering.

00:00:17.290 --> 00:00:24.540
Just unconscionable numbers when we total
up the job losses from these nine or so weeks

00:00:24.540 --> 00:00:29.670
of corona virus unemployment, pandemic area
era.

00:00:29.670 --> 00:00:31.780
Maybe it's 10 weeks at this point, something
like that.

00:00:31.780 --> 00:00:37.520
We are now at thirty eight point six million
jobs lost.

00:00:37.520 --> 00:00:40.850
If on February 1st, anybody had said.

00:00:40.850 --> 00:00:45.519
My prediction is by June 1st, we will have
lost 40 million jobs, which is what it will

00:00:45.519 --> 00:00:48.809
probably be after we add another week or two
of numbers.

00:00:48.809 --> 00:00:50.550
I don't think anybody would have believed
it.

00:00:50.550 --> 00:00:57.000
Now, if you want to find some kind of like
it could be worse type of thing in the numbers,

00:00:57.000 --> 00:01:02.870
what we can point to is at least the numbers
are declining each week in terms of new people

00:01:02.870 --> 00:01:05.770
losing their jobs in early April.

00:01:05.770 --> 00:01:11.369
Each week we had like more than 6 million
people filed for new unemployment benefits.

00:01:11.369 --> 00:01:17.149
And then by the end of April, early May, we
were at 4 million a week, 3 million a week.

00:01:17.149 --> 00:01:25.659
And it sounds crazy, but that number is now
down to only 2.4 million more jobs lost last

00:01:25.659 --> 00:01:26.659
week.

00:01:26.659 --> 00:01:31.509
So in a sense, we're seeing a decreased rate
of people losing their jobs.

00:01:31.509 --> 00:01:37.330
But once again, even with these diminishing
numbers, by mid-July, we could be at 50 million,

00:01:37.330 --> 00:01:39.750
50 million people having lost their jobs.

00:01:39.750 --> 00:01:43.869
Now, let's do a little bit of context, because
this is important when we talk about this.

00:01:43.869 --> 00:01:49.749
The total workforce before the pandemic hit
was estimated at one hundred and fifty eight

00:01:49.749 --> 00:01:50.749
million people.

00:01:50.749 --> 00:01:53.960
So the country has three hundred and thirty
one million people.

00:01:53.960 --> 00:01:54.980
Many of those are children.

00:01:54.980 --> 00:01:56.179
Many of those are retired people.

00:01:56.179 --> 00:02:02.220
So the active working labor force was believed
to be one hundred and fifty eight million

00:02:02.220 --> 00:02:03.220
people.

00:02:03.220 --> 00:02:09.690
So thirty eight point six million losing their
jobs means that twenty four point four.

00:02:09.690 --> 00:02:11.550
Call it 25 percent.

00:02:11.550 --> 00:02:18.320
About a quarter of the American workforce
lost jobs during the Corona virus pandemic.

00:02:18.320 --> 00:02:21.680
Now, we've got to adjust those numbers a little
bit.

00:02:21.680 --> 00:02:23.460
One way a little bit the other way.

00:02:23.460 --> 00:02:27.770
One reality is these are probably underestimates
because there are lots of people who lost

00:02:27.770 --> 00:02:32.100
jobs, but for one reason or another didn't
file unemployment benefits.

00:02:32.100 --> 00:02:38.140
There may be people who lost a job, but they're
independently wealthy or have another source

00:02:38.140 --> 00:02:39.140
of income or whatever.

00:02:39.140 --> 00:02:42.140
And they just decided I'm not going to go
through the hassle of filing unemployment.

00:02:42.140 --> 00:02:43.850
That's a small group of people.

00:02:43.850 --> 00:02:49.690
You have a larger group of people who would
file an unemployment claim, except it's what

00:02:49.690 --> 00:02:54.370
we might call a marginal job or a job that
is under the table, in which case you wouldn't

00:02:54.370 --> 00:02:56.580
be able to file for unemployment benefits.

00:02:56.580 --> 00:02:59.790
And there are still some fraction of people
who have just been struggling to get the claim

00:02:59.790 --> 00:03:02.700
in because systems have been overloaded.

00:03:02.700 --> 00:03:05.730
Unemployment is run at the state level, 50
states, 50 different systems.

00:03:05.730 --> 00:03:08.950
Some are mostly phone, some are online combination
of the two.

00:03:08.950 --> 00:03:13.030
So in a sense, these numbers are probably
too low now.

00:03:13.030 --> 00:03:21.630
On the other side of this, there are people
who lost jobs early on but have found new

00:03:21.630 --> 00:03:22.630
jobs.

00:03:22.630 --> 00:03:26.460
So we have a cumulative total of thirty eight
point six million unemployment claims in the

00:03:26.460 --> 00:03:28.500
last nine, 10, 11 weeks.

00:03:28.500 --> 00:03:32.300
But some of those people who lost their job
in March, for example, have found a new job.

00:03:32.300 --> 00:03:36.380
We don't know exactly what number that is,
but there are businesses that have been hiring

00:03:36.380 --> 00:03:41.720
delivery, business based businesses, grocery
stores, places like Amazon and others have

00:03:41.720 --> 00:03:42.720
been hiring.

00:03:42.720 --> 00:03:45.600
So some of the people that lost jobs have
found new jobs.

00:03:45.600 --> 00:03:49.890
Now, what we can be sure of is given this
bad news, the stock market is going to do

00:03:49.890 --> 00:03:50.890
really, really well today.

00:03:50.890 --> 00:03:53.060
And I'm sort of kidding.

00:03:53.060 --> 00:03:56.540
But we've seen a stock market that's increasingly
divorced from the unemployment news.

00:03:56.540 --> 00:04:03.080
Now, as we've talked about before, stock markets
often react to how the news compares to expectations.

00:04:03.080 --> 00:04:07.740
So these new numbers of about two point forty
four million more people losing their jobs

00:04:07.740 --> 00:04:08.920
last week.

00:04:08.920 --> 00:04:11.290
It's more or less in line with expectations.

00:04:11.290 --> 00:04:14.280
So it may not move the market very, very much.

00:04:14.280 --> 00:04:22.610
So what we may actually see really shake up
the stock market would be the numbers that

00:04:22.610 --> 00:04:25.790
we're going to get about the second quarter
in total.

00:04:25.790 --> 00:04:33.330
So Q2 earnings and Q2 GDP may be numbers that
really move the stock market potentially down,

00:04:33.330 --> 00:04:34.330
but maybe not.

00:04:34.330 --> 00:04:35.870
So first you've got earnings seasons.

00:04:35.870 --> 00:04:39.700
How much did companies make during the second
quarter?

00:04:39.700 --> 00:04:44.850
And typically we get those numbers for the
second quarter, which is April, May, June,

00:04:44.850 --> 00:04:49.340
around July 15th, we'll start to get earnings
numbers from companies.

00:04:49.340 --> 00:04:50.960
And that may push the market up.

00:04:50.960 --> 00:04:52.870
It may pull the market down.

00:04:52.870 --> 00:04:55.340
Then we will get GDP numbers even later on.

00:04:55.340 --> 00:04:59.570
So we'll get initial GDP estimates for the
second quarter on July 30th.

00:04:59.570 --> 00:05:03.970
And then we'll get more accurate second quarter
GDP estimates towards the end of August, so

00:05:03.970 --> 00:05:07.660
realize that we are not going to know the
full scope of what happened in this quarter

00:05:07.660 --> 00:05:12.700
right now essentially until mid to late August,
almost September.

00:05:12.700 --> 00:05:18.690
In practice, there are predictions that we
could see a 20 percent GDP decline in Q2 25

00:05:18.690 --> 00:05:19.690
percent.

00:05:19.690 --> 00:05:21.210
One estimate said 35 percent.

00:05:21.210 --> 00:05:22.840
That's certainly on the very high end.

00:05:22.840 --> 00:05:25.010
So this is about suffering.

00:05:25.010 --> 00:05:26.740
It's not about politics.

00:05:26.740 --> 00:05:28.240
But we can't ignore that.

00:05:28.240 --> 00:05:32.810
Historically bad GDP numbers, the worst GDP
numbers the country has ever seen.

00:05:32.810 --> 00:05:36.510
Eight weeks before a presidential election,
we will know the full scope of those those

00:05:36.510 --> 00:05:38.980
numbers again, late August.

00:05:38.980 --> 00:05:41.050
That may be very, very bad for Donald Trump.

00:05:41.050 --> 00:05:45.780
Now, for now, as states have been improving
on their coronavirus numbers and putting in

00:05:45.780 --> 00:05:50.330
place plans to get some portions of their
state economies back to work.

00:05:50.330 --> 00:05:52.870
My hope is because, again, for me, this is
not political.

00:05:52.870 --> 00:05:58.960
I want things to improve economically and
medically as quickly as is reasonably possible.

00:05:58.960 --> 00:06:02.030
Some portions of people are going to be getting
back to work.

00:06:02.030 --> 00:06:06.000
Hopefully things will tick up a bit or at
least at least continue ticking down less.

00:06:06.000 --> 00:06:09.970
And reminder that the most important takeaway
here, we want to take what's going on and

00:06:09.970 --> 00:06:12.370
turn it into action for the long term.

00:06:12.370 --> 00:06:15.389
We've got to fix the problem now, of course,
over the long term.

00:06:15.389 --> 00:06:21.590
The lesson here is even economies that look
really good by all of the traditional indicators

00:06:21.590 --> 00:06:24.290
are really, really fragile.

00:06:24.290 --> 00:06:27.330
Our lack of preparedness has been exposed
here.

00:06:27.330 --> 00:06:28.680
There will be more pandemics.

00:06:28.680 --> 00:06:29.980
There are going to be more downturns.

00:06:29.980 --> 00:06:31.550
We can't control that.

00:06:31.550 --> 00:06:35.530
What we can control is the underlying fragility
of our economic system.

00:06:35.530 --> 00:06:36.521
And it's been exposed as very, very fragile.

